INSERT INTO `blog_entry_text` VALUES (1927,'<p>PHOENIX, ARIZONA (05/15/06) &#8211; Software Sensation has released version 2.5 of jWebApp which \r\nis a Model-View-Controller web development framework. It is a Java based solution and is \r\nreported to be extremely simple, yet is more powerful and feature rich than any other \r\nframework. jWebApp uses a very simple XML configuration to associate web requests with Java \r\nclass objects and requires only knowledge with Java, your business objects, and a view \r\ntechnology such as HTML matched with JSP, Velocity, WebMacro, or anything else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Software Sensation has managed to create a framework that does not require proprietary tags \r\nor extensive APIs, which in turn allows the jWebApp controller to be very small. Programmers \r\nand information technology groups will find jWebApp extremely easy to work with, and will \r\ndefinitely see a dramatic decrease in development time and effort.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The result of the small controller gives jWebApp the ability to be the fastest server software \r\navailable in the framework arena. Software Sensation invites you to compare jWebApp with any \r\nserver development software. Because jWebApp is something that can be learned and fully understood \r\nin a matter of hours, there&#8217;s no need to search for programmers with several years of experience. \r\njWebApp is perfect for business web application development.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp includes built-in support for role-level security, both application and container based \r\nauthentication, secure socket (https) management, request forwarding and redirects, request scripting \r\nwith any BSF-compatible scripting language, url-rewriting for cookie-limited browsers, application \r\nand XML configuration based data validation, and much more. jWebApp also allows for the choice of \r\nany model and view software needed, including: JDO, Hibernate, JDBC, JSP/JSTL/EL, Velocity, WebMacro, \r\nand anything else.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Software Sensation has been incorporated since 2001, and develops client/server and website software \r\nfor open systems platforms (UNIX/Windows) using C, C++, and Java. Software Sensation has several \r\nproducts on the market, including Install&Update, and the Memory Structures Library for C/C++.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp is reasonably priced at just $499, and includes a year of free updates and online support. \r\njWebApp can also be used free for an indefinite period of time with unlimited temporary licensing.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>CONTACT:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>David Bulmore</p>\r\n\r\n<p>602-206-9801</p>\r\nNeBY3XlqdECOY5vwSxaEEuGb9mHClgrTVEJbRtX2LENXMJKCdtZGucOU3LqW','2008-09-07 00:38:10'),(1928,'<p>jPersist is an extremely powerful, light-weight, object-relational database persistence API with automatic \r\nmapping. jPersist uses/wraps JDBC and can work with any relational database and any type of connection \r\nresource. jPersist uses information obtained from the database (DatabaseMetaData) to handle mapping between \r\nthe database and Java objects, so mapping configuration and annotation are not needed; in fact, there is no \r\nconfiguration needed at all.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jPersist has a database manager class that handles pooling of its own resources as well as managing \r\nJDBC connections as needed. The database manager provides several object-relational access methods \r\nthat allow queries, inserts, updates, deletes, etc. in a single line of code.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>However, the real power of jPersist resides in the jpersist.Database class. This class is a powerhouse \r\nof functionality that provides object-oriented access to your relational database. jPersist is completely \r\nflexible and can be used with true POJO objects, or extended so your objects can provide object-relational \r\ndatabase abilities, and jPersist can even be cast to an interface for database proxying.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jPersist also implements ListIterator to provide forward and backward traversal of the data returned \r\n(provided the current result is not TYPE_FORWARD_ONLY). This also allows jPersist to be used directly \r\nin page rendering tools such as JSP and Velocity. jPersist also easily handles all types of data \r\nassociations and inheritance (single table inheritance, and multi-table join inheritance).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jPersist is as easy as:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class Customer extends PersistentObject { ... }\r\n\r\n    DatabaseManager dbm = DatabaseManager.getDatabaseManager(...);\r\n\r\n    Customer customer = (Customer)dbm.loadObject(new Customer(&#8220;Smith&#8221;));\r\n\r\n    customer.setLastName(&#8220;Smithman&#8221;);\r\n    dbm.saveObject(customer);\r\n\r\n    dbm.saveObject(new Customer(\"dbuser1\", \"passwd\", \"Dave\", \"B.\"));\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>or with the jpersist.Database class:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    Database db = dbm.getDatabase();\r\n    Customer customer = new Customer();\r\n\r\n    customer.setCustomerId(&#8220;tnug%&#8221;);\r\n    db.queryObject(customer);\r\n\r\n    while (db.hasNext() && db.next() != null)\r\n    {\r\n        System.out.println(db.loadObject(customer));\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    db.close();\r\n</pre>\r\nN2PIkyC1V5wU4QF2Oq5DowdURXFDpEM59yoQWNBAe3W1w4H5Sd8lkOj4oWLZ','2008-09-07 00:40:20'),(1929,'<p>jWebApp is a Model-View-Controller framework that is simple and straight forward and doesn&#8217;t \r\ntry to be all things. In fact, jWebApp is specifically designed to satisfy the need for simpler web \r\ndevelopment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp provides support for all of the features you expect from a framework, but does not need \r\ntag libraries or extensive APIs to implement them. This allows jWebApp to actually attain the \r\nseparation of concerns and allows independence in model and view technologies. You can use any \r\nmodel/business layer technologies, any database-access technologies, any view-rendering technologies, \r\nand plain old HTML and HTML forms (or what ever you like).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp Is Configuration Free</p>\r\n\r\n<p>With jWebApp, you have two modes of use that can be used interchangeably. The first mode is a \r\nconfiguration free mode where you simply drive the web application with URIs. jWebApp automatically \r\nfinds the Java objects required to satisfy a request. For example with the following URL:<p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n     http://www.site.com/application/HelloWorld\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp, by default, will find a class named webapp.HelloWorld.class. jWebApp will automatically load \r\nthe class and call the appropriate methods. The URI can be anything as long as all or part of it \r\nidentifies a locatable class. All of jWebApps functionality is available in code and the methods only \r\nneed to return a forwarding URI, which can be another request handler or a final destination such as a \r\nJSP or Velocity page.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp also comes with jPersist, which is an extremely powerful, object-relational database persistence \r\nAPI that manages to avoid the need for configuration and annotation; POJO mapping is automatic. jPersist \r\nuses JDBC and can work with any relational database and any type of connection resource.</p>ES6HPAbin0p6fduyPMTIqDrLG3H904MqbE2ixOwHDgziIKw1VcxB1lsWoK3a','2008-09-07 00:43:43'),(1930,'<p>This release marks the final changes for a stable/production release. \r\nA ton of testing has taken place and all supported platforms have been \r\ntested. jWebApp is now production ready and moving from beta to final \r\nrelease.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Release Notes:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n    <li>Factored out the main controller</li>\r\n    <li>Fixed javadoc issue</li>\r\n    <li>Added handling for mapping URLs to methods</li>\r\n    <li>Some simple find bug and PMD fixes</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Features:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n    <li>Configuration Free Web Applications</li>\r\n    <li>Model-View-Controller architecture</li>\r\n    <li>Extremely small learning curve (learn in minutes)</li>\r\n    <li>All the functionality offered by other frameworks and more</li>\r\n    <li>No proprietary tag libraries and no need for any</li>\r\n    <li>Use JSP/JSTL/Velocity or whatever you like</li>\r\n    <li>Use regular HTML and Forms</li>\r\n    <li>Optionally use any BSF scripting language</li>\r\n    <li>Extensive validation support for your data</li>\r\n    <li>Easily handle forwarding, redirects, and non-secure connections</li>\r\n    <li>Easily handle SSL(https)</li>\r\n    <li>Role level security</li>\r\n    <li>Container and application based authentication</li>\r\n    <li>Built-in URL rewriting for handling cookie-limited browsers</li>\r\n    <li>Multipart forms and file uploads</li>\r\n    <li>Email with attachments and HTML</li>\r\n    <li>Payment processing</li>\r\n    <li>Support for regular expression matching</li>\r\n    <li>Secure file downloading and uploading</li>\r\n    <li>and tons more (includes jWebTk and JPersist)</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nujsXvRzTeVFJASp3UYrkwgcSDNCQK8T9s4mokl563nwM15xD9v2Zjq7gfhimWLXeKFf32Nd29ltiUyDr','2008-09-07 00:52:32'),(1931,'<p>This release marks the final changes for a stable/production release. \r\nA ton of testing has taken place and all supported platforms have \r\nbeen tested. jPersist is now production ready and moving from beta \r\nto final release.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Release Notes:</p>\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Fixed possible null pointer error, pointed out by findbugs</li>\r\n<li>Numerous changes, including moving mysql generated key processing to the other \"get last key\" mode</li>\r\n<li>Refactoring of names, renamed ConvertObject to convertObject</li>\r\n<li>Renamed DatabaseManager.loadObject() to DatabaseManager.queryAndLoadObject()</li>\r\n<li>Added handling for setting null values in the database when the getXXX method returns null</li>\r\n<li>Added support for prefix/suffix stripping</li>\r\n<li>Some simple find bug and PMD fixes</li>\r\n<li>Added the null handling implementation to the protected members of jpersist.Database</li>\r\n<li>Also fixed a small but major error with not cloning the database handler during updates</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p>Features:</p>\r\n\r\n<li>Object-oriented relational database access </li>\r\n<li>Object-oriented queries, inserts, updates, deletes </li>\r\n<li>Object-oriented iteration through data </li>\r\n<li>Easily create and load associations </li>\r\n<li>Supports any JDBC source including JNDI </li>\r\n<li>Uses JDBC with access to underlying connections, statements and result sets </li>\r\n<li>Stored procedures and prepared statements with the same object-oriented functionality </li>\r\n<li>manage multiple result sets </li>\r\n<li>Implements ListIterator for forward/backward traversal </li>\r\n<li>Can be used directly in page rendering </li>\r\n<li>Tracks changes to objects for updating </li>\r\n<li>jPersist.Database can be used as is or extended </li>\r\n<li>jPersist.Database can also be cast to an interface for database proxying </li>\r\n<li>Multiple utilities for loading data in to collections and maps </li>\r\n<li>and far more!</li>\r\nxNhRs7eVlo032it4JQ8ThTwYXxVdkjsT2JCegV6ODZX2GpJdlBnHLZNpz5tV','2008-09-07 00:55:29'),(1932,'<p>jWebApp is a trouble free full stack MVC web application framework that \r\ntruly removes the complexities of web development and its configuration. \r\njWebApp is so simple, it can actually be learned in a matter of minutes. \r\nThis release includes added support for URL to object mapping, forms, \r\nand RESTful web services.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp Features:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n    <li>An extremely easy web application framework</li>\r\n    <li>Full stack web development</li>\r\n    <li>Model-View-Controller architecture</li>\r\n    <li>Incredibly easy server side AJAX support</li>\r\n    <li>Full support for RESTful web services</li>\r\n    <li>Simple configuration free web applications</li>\r\n    <li>Easy-to-use URL to object mapping</li>\r\n    <li>Extremely small learning curve (learn in minutes)</li>\r\n    <li>All the functionality offered by other frameworks and more</li>\r\n    <li>No proprietary tag libraries and no need for any</li>\r\n    <li>Use JSP/JSTL, Velocity, or whatever you like</li>\r\n    <li>Use regular HTML and Forms</li>\r\n    <li>Optionally use any BSF compatible scripting language</li>\r\n    <li>Extensive validation support for your data</li>\r\n    <li>Easily handle forwarding, redirects, and non-secure connections</li>\r\n    <li>Easily handle SSL(https)</li>\r\n    <li>Role level security</li>\r\n    <li>Container- and application-based authentication</li>\r\n    <li>Built-in URL rewriting for handling cookie-limited browsers</li>\r\n    <li>Multipart forms and file uploads</li>\r\n    <li>Email with attachments and HTML</li>\r\n    <li>Backend direct payment processing</li>\r\n    <li>Support for regular expression matching</li>\r\n    <li>Secure file downloading and uploading</li>\r\n    <li>and tons more (with jCommonTk, jWebTk and JPersist)</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp is literally this simple:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class JWebAppExample extends RequestHandler \r\n      {\r\n        DatabaseManager jpersistdb = AppUtils.getDatabaseManager();\r\n    \r\n        public String processGetCustomer(HttpServletRequest request) \r\n          {\r\n            request.setAttribute(\"customer\", jpersistdb.loadObject(Customer.class,\r\n                                          \"where :customerId like ?\", \r\n                                          request.getParameter(&#8220;customerId&#8221;)));\r\n            \r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/customer.jsp\";\r\n          }\r\n    \r\n        public String validateNewCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface)\r\n          {\r\n            ... // trivial validation stuff\r\n            if (errors.length() &gt; 0) return &#8220;/WEB-INF/newCustomer.jsp&#8221;\r\n            else return SUCCESS;\r\n          }\r\n    \r\n        public String processNewCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface) \r\n          {\r\n            Customer customer = new Customer();\r\n    \r\n            serverInterface.fillObjectFromRequest(customer);\r\n    \r\n            jpersistdb.saveObject(customer);\r\n    \r\n            serverInterface.setAttribute(&#8220;customer&#8221;, customer);\r\n            serverInterface.setAttribute(&#8220;customerSaved&#8221;, &#8220;Customer Saved&#8221;);\r\n    \r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/customer.jsp\";\r\n          }\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>JSP:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    &lt;div align=\"center\"&gt;\r\n        &lt;h2&gt;jWebApp Example.&lt;/h2&gt;\r\n        &lt;b&gt;${customerSaved}&lt;/b&gt;\r\n        &lt;p&gt;CustomerId: ${customer.customerId}\r\n        &lt;p&gt;First Name: ${customer.firstName}\r\n        &lt;p&gt;Last Name: ${customer.lastName}\r\n        ...\r\n    &lt;/div&gt;\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>and calling:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    host/context/jWebAppExample/getCustomer?customerId=value\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>or post a form with:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    host/context/jWebAppExample/newCustomer\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>Using the above given URLs, jWebApp automatically finds the supporting class, instantiates \r\nit, and executes the supporting method. jWebApp does not require any configuration or annotation, \r\nso the above is actually all you have to do.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp provides support for all of the features you expect, but does not need additional tag \r\nlibraries or extensive APIs to implement them, what you already know is all you will need. With \r\njWebApp, you can use any model/business layer technologies, any database-access technologies, \r\nany web-authoring technologies, and plain old HTML and HTML forms.</p>aYIT2HbQwFpPb4hWwyaEBHpyGSCn9ZjbwugLms6C3rXg5vaciQ0mQJaB6CMN0Tmc65wxKIhPUwx4J26I','2008-09-07 00:58:34'),(1933,'<p>jPersist now includes added support for more efficient use of prepared statements \r\nand batch updates, and fixes a couple of issues introduced with yesterday&#8217;s \r\njWebApp release.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>With jPersist you can now do the following:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    // Inserting contacts - efficiently uses a single prepared statement\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact0\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact1\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact2\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact3\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact4\", ...));\r\n\r\n    /**\r\n     * Inserting contacts - uses batch updating\r\n     * and efficiently uses a single prepared statement\r\n     */\r\n    db.beginBatch();\r\n\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact5\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact6\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact7\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact8\", ...));\r\n    db.saveObject(new Contact(\"contact9\", ...));\r\n\r\n    db.executeBatch();\r\n    db.endBatch();\r\n\r\n    /**\r\n     * The following are equivalent to the above.\r\n     */\r\n\r\n    // Inserting contacts - efficiently uses a single prepared statement\r\n    new UpdateManager(dbm) \r\n      {\r\n        public void run() throws JPersistException \r\n          {\r\n            for (int i = 10; i &lt; 500; i++)\r\n              saveObject(new Contact(\"contact\" + i, ...));\r\n          }\r\n      }.executeUpdates();\r\n\r\n    /**\r\n     * Inserting contacts - uses batch updating\r\n     * and efficiently uses a single prepared statement\r\n     */\r\n    new UpdateManager(dbm) \r\n      {\r\n        public void run() throws JPersistException \r\n          {\r\n            for (int i = 501; i &lt; 1000; i++)\r\n              saveObject(new Contact(\"contact\" + i, ...));\r\n          }\r\n      }.executeBatchUpdates();\r\n</pre>\r\n          \r\n<p>jPersist is an extremely powerful object-relational persistence API that is based on the \r\nActive-Record and Data-Mapper patterns. jPersist wraps JDBC functionality and can work with \r\nany relational database, and any type of connection resource, including: java.sql.DriverManager, \r\nJNDI, javax.sql.DataSource, and third party connection pooling libraries (DBCP, C3PO, etc.).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Using jPersist is as simple as:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public void DatabaseExample(DatabaseManager dbm) throws JPersistException\r\n      {\r\n        // Inserting contact with associations\r\n        Contact contact = new Contact(\"deisenhower\", ...);\r\n\r\n        contact.getSupport().add(new Support(\"Request\", ...));\r\n        contact.getSupport().add(new Support(\"Response\", ...));\r\n        contact.getSupport().add(new Support(\"Request\", ...));\r\n\r\n        contact.getOrders().add(new Order(\"Dwight D. Eisenhower Dollar\", ...));\r\n        contact.getOrders().add(new Order(\"Susan B. Anthony Dollar\", ...));\r\n\r\n        // Saving within an automatic transaction (covers all relationships)\r\n        dbm.saveObject(contact);\r\n\r\n        // Load based on information contained in classes\r\n        contact = dbm.loadObject(Contact.class, \"where :contactId like ?\", &#8220;tjef%&#8221;);\r\n        System.out.println(\"\\ncontactId = \" + contact.getContactId());\r\n\r\n        // Load a collection of objects from the database\r\n        Collection&lt;Contact&gt; c = dbm.loadObjects(new Vector&lt;Contact&gt;(), Contact.class);\r\n\r\n        for (Contact contact2 : c)\r\n          System.out.println(\"contactId = \" + contact2.getContactId());\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>jPersist Features:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n    <li>Object-relational persistence API</li>\r\n    <li>A Java implementation of the active-record and data-mapper patterns</li>\r\n    <li>Mapping is automatic, there is no configuration, or annotation required</li>\r\n    <li>Objects are isolated from database changes, and vice versa</li>\r\n    <li>Optionally provides simple annotation/functional support for mapping</li>\r\n    <li>Full Support for MySQL, DB2, Oracle, Derby, HSQL, PostgreSQL, H2, and more</li>\r\n    <li>Object-oriented queries, inserts, updates, deletes and more</li>\r\n    <li>All JDBC functionality is supported at some level</li>\r\n    <li>Intermix objects with SQL, and use object properties in SQL fragments</li>\r\n    <li>Full support for database transactions (automatic and manual)</li>\r\n    <li>Automatically handles inheritance via table joins</li>\r\n    <li>Also supports single table inheritance and concrete table inheritance</li>\r\n    <li>Easily handles associations with single instance, arrays, and collections</li>\r\n    <li>Supports any JDBC source including DriverManager, JNDI, and DataSource</li>\r\n    <li>Support for third-party connection pooling (DBCP, C3PO, etc.)</li>\r\n    <li>No SQL required for queries, inserts, updates, deletes</li>\r\n    <li>Uses JDBC with access to underlying connection, statement and result set </li>\r\n    <li>Access stored procedures and prepared statements with the same object-oriented functionality </li>\r\n    <li>Full support for batch updates</li>\r\n    <li>Implements ListIterator and Iterable for object-oriented iteration through data </li>\r\n    <li>Tracks object persistence for inserting and updating </li>\r\n    <li>Can be cast to an interface for database proxying </li>\r\n    <li>Multiple utilities for loading data into collections and maps </li>\r\n    <li>and more</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>All in all, jPersist&#8217;s sole reason for being is to provide simple trouble-free POJO oriented\r\naccess to your database. With jPersist and your POJOs, you should rarely have to\r\ndo anything more than:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Define a POJO (optionally extending jpersist.PersistentObject), which can be extended by other POJOs \r\n(relational hierarchy via joins). Call one of jpersist.DatabaseManager&#8217;s: loadObject(), saveObject(), \r\nand deleteObject(), or one of jpersist.Database&#8217;s: saveObject(), deleteObject(), and queryObject() with \r\njpersist.Result&#8217;s loadObject().  Group saves and deletes in a transaction using jpersist.TransactionManager \r\nor use jpersist.Database&#8217;s transaction oriented methods.  Group updates in an update manager using \r\njpersist.UpdateManager or use jpersist.Database&#8217;s update and batch oriented methods. That&#8217;s not to say you \r\nwon&#8217;t have other JDBC needs, like calling stored procedures or executing SQL statements, but with jPersist \r\nit&#8217;s all easy to handle.</p>\r\nO4AHJB0cFKtjUO0Dn8Nah0jAZC8FzI6Kv3dalFTSYNzgDhcqAJ42yE0LZWdg','2008-09-07 01:03:33'),(1934,'<p>jWebApp and jPersist provide a simple alternative to other Java frameworks and \r\nRAILs, but don\'t let the following fool you, there is a ton of functionality \r\nprovided with both jWebApp and jPersist.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp and jPersist make web development as easy as defining a simple Java class:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class Customer extends RequestHandler\r\n      {\r\n        public String processGetCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface) \r\n          {\r\n            String customerId = serverInterface.getParameter(&#8220;customerId&#8221;);\r\n            Customer customer = jpersist.loadObject(new Customer(customerId));\r\n\r\n            serverInterface.setAttribute(\"customer\", customer);\r\n            \r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/customer.jsp\";\r\n          }\r\n      } \r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>and creating a view with plain HTML and your favorite template markup (JSP, Velocity, etc):</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    &lt;div align=\"center\"&gt;\r\n        &lt;h3&gt;Customer ${customer.firstName}, ${customer.lastName}&lt;/h3&gt;\r\n    ...\r\n    &lt;/div&gt;\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>and then calling:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    http://host/context/customer/getCustomer?customerId=jsmith\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>The following servlet configuration is all that is needed:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    &lt;servlet&gt;\r\n        &lt;servlet-name&gt;jwaRequestServlet&lt;/servlet-name&gt;\r\n        &lt;servlet-class&gt;jwebapp.RequestServlet&lt;/servlet-class&gt;\r\n    &lt;/servlet&gt;\r\n    \r\n    &lt;servlet-mapping&gt;\r\n        &lt;servlet-name&gt;jwaRequestServlet&lt;/servlet-name&gt;\r\n        &lt;url-pattern&gt;/customer/*&lt;/url-pattern&gt;\r\n    &lt;/servlet-mapping&gt;\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>That\'s it! No other XML, annotation, or configuration is needed!</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Is there an easier way?</p>ndfBvk1i9jEsJZPpj9U4rnaKWJpNEDCvf07WdjZOXBP7MH2wrkKje2RyvfYH','2008-09-07 01:05:54'),(1935,'<p>jWebApp is among the easiest web frameworks to learn and use, and allows for \r\nextremely fast web application development.  By default, jWebApp uses a simple \r\nURI to object mapping controller, providing a totally configuration and annotation \r\nfree mode.  jWebApp also supports a simple XML based configuration that allows for \r\nobject reuse, regular expression matching of URIs, configuration based forwarding, \r\nredirects, validation, role based security, SSL, error handling and a lot more.  \r\nYou can simply use JSP/JSTL, Velocity, etc., along with plain HTML and HTML forms.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If we were to ask ourselves &#8220;what are we actually doing with a web application&#8221;, the answer \r\nwould be something like &#8220;we are associating application code with a web request&#8221;. Putting it \r\nanother way we are associating application code with a URL and an HTTP method (GET or POST). \r\nSo, in the end, all our web framework really needs to do is reliably map a URL:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler/viewCustomer?customerId=value\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>to an object and method:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class CustomerHandler extends RequestHandler\r\n      {\r\n        public String processViewCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface)\r\n          {\r\n            String customerId = serverInterface.getParameter(&#8220;customerId&#8221;);\r\n\r\n            serverInterface.setAttribute(\"customer\", jpersist.loadObject(new Customer(customerId));\r\n\r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/customer.jsp\";\r\n          }\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>jWebApp provides the shortest path from your web URL to application code without any need \r\nfor configuration or annotation. As an example, if you have a customer oriented web site and \r\nhave a means for registering new customers, you might have a request handler that handles \r\nsaving and viewing the customer&#8217;s information:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class CustomerHandler extends RequestHandler\r\n      {\r\n        // processRequest is the default when no method is included\r\n        public String processRequest(ServerInterface serverInterface) {...}\r\n        public String processViewCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface) {...}\r\n\r\n        public String validateSaveCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface) {...}\r\n        public String processSaveCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface) {...}\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>With jWebApp, to access the methods you would use the following URLs:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    // the default\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler/saveCustomer\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler/viewCustomer?customerId=value\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>The URL contains the class name optionally followed by the method name:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    http://host/[context/]className[/methodName]\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>or, for our purposes:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler[/saveCustomer]\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>and, it actually is just that simple! jWebApp goes to great lengths to handle finding \r\nthe application code needed to satisfy the request. You can define all or part of the \r\nclasspath for the class in the URL, and there can be any number of methods defined in \r\nthe class for any number of mappings. Mappings must be preceded with &#8220;validate&#8221; or \r\n&#8220;process&#8221;, so no methods can be called that aren&#8217;t callable. Classes can also be defined \r\nas part of a package (defined in the web.xml file) so that only classes in the package \r\ncan be called, and jWebApp also allows that amount of the classpath to be omitted from \r\nthe URL. Once the class and method is found, it is executed and cached for future calls.\r\n URL&#8217;s can also contain extra path information either before the class/method or \r\n after. jWebApp also supports all forms of servlet mapping (path, extension, exact, etc).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>What is done in the mapped method is entirely up to the developer. In most cases we \r\nare either saving user entered data or loading data to be presented to a user. jWebApp \r\nprovides a ton of functionality to make the developers life easier, and because jWebApp \r\nisn&#8217;t all controlling, the developer is free to use any other tools (APIs, taglibs, etc).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>You may also have noticed that jWebApp is naturally RESTful.  With jWebApp, you can use \r\nthe same configuration and annotation free URI mapping controller to handle REST based web \r\nservices using any of the HTTP verbs that are used with REST (GET, HEAD, PUT, POST, DELETE, \r\nOPTIONS).  jWebApp also supports a more formal REST mode by extending RestfulHandler and\r\nimplementing the HTTP verbs.  Either way, you are RESTful.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In order to provide a more detailed example, we will also be using jPersist (included with jWebApp).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>We&#8217;ll define a class to handle our customer&#8217;s information:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class Customer\r\n      {\r\n        String customerId, firstName, lastName, address1, address2, city, state, country, phone, email;\r\n\r\n        public String getCustomerId() { return customerId; }\r\n        public void setCustomerId(String _customerId) { customerId = _customerId; }\r\n        ...\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>it is a simple POJO with attributes and getters/setters. jPersist is POJO oriented as well as persistence \r\noriented (by simply extending PersistentObject). To setup a jPersist database manager, simply use one of the \r\nDatabaseManager&#8217;s factory methods. jWebApp provides servlet initialization and destruction methods which are \r\ndefined with the servlet in the web.xml file.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Our application setup is simple:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class AppUtils extends InitDestroyHandler\r\n      {\r\n        private static DatabaseManager dbManager;\r\n\r\n        public void init(ServletContext context)\r\n          {\r\n            dbManager = DatabaseManager.getJndiDefinedDatabaseManager(\"contacts\", 15, \r\n                                                                      \"jdbc/contacts\", null, null);\r\n          }\r\n\r\n        public static DatabaseManager getDatabaseManager() { return dbManager; }\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>The above is all that is needed by either jWebApp or jPersist (you have to define your \r\nJNDI resource, but that&#8217;s a servlet thing).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Now our URLs (HTTP requests) as defined above:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler/saveCustomer\r\n    http://host/[context/]customerHandler/viewCustomer?customerId=value\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>map to the following class and methods:</p>\r\n\r\n<pre>\r\n    public class CustomerHandler extends RequestHandler\r\n      {\r\n        // AppUtils is initialized before any requests are handled.\r\n        DatabaseManager dbm = AppUtils.getDatabaseManager();\r\n\r\n        // We can have a default request: http://host/[context/]customerHandler\r\n        public String processRequest(ServerInterface serverInterface)\r\n          {\r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/customer.jsp\"; // send the user to a page/form\r\n          }\r\n\r\n        // Load customer information based on customerId          \r\n        public String processViewCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface)\r\n          {\r\n            String customerId = serverInterface.getParameter(&#8220;customerId&#8221;);\r\n\r\n            // jPersist builds a JDBC query based on your object and its data\r\n            serverInterface.setAttribute(\"customer\", dbm.loadObject(new Customer(customerId));\r\n\r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/customer.jsp\";\r\n          }\r\n\r\n        // Any method can have a validation method associated with it.\r\n        public String validateSaveCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface)\r\n          {\r\n            // If validation fails send an error message and return to a web page.\r\n            // Otherwise:\r\n            return SUCCESS;\r\n          }\r\n\r\n        // If the validation method returns SUCCESS, then the process method is called.\r\n        public String processSaveCustomer(ServerInterface serverInterface)\r\n          {\r\n            Customer customer = new Customer();\r\n\r\n            // automatically fill an object with the request data matches property values.\r\n            serverInterface.fillObjectFromRequest(customer);\r\n\r\n            dbm.saveObject(customer);\r\n\r\n            return \"/WEB-INF/orders.jsp\";\r\n          }\r\n      }\r\n</pre>\r\n\r\n<p>Your request mapped methods can forward anywhere any number of times, but at some point \r\nthey need to return an address to a page renderer such as JSP (*.jsp) or Velocity (*.vm), \r\nor return NO_FORWARDING if there is no response to be sent.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Of course, with any framework there&#8217;s always a lot more to it then is presented in a short \r\narticle, and this is true with jWebApp as well. But, what you see here is pretty well all you \r\nneed to know to develop complex database driven web applications.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>With jWebApp there&#8217;s no configuration required, no XML, no annotations, and no additional \r\n(proprietary) tag libraries. You can always use simple HTML and HTML forms and anything else \r\nyou like. And for presentation you can use JSP/JSTL, Velocity, or anything you like here as \r\nwell.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In short, with jWebApp, what you already know is actually all you need to know.</p>ljkXpo0EstjGJ5MPrkNF2SDt0o9YzXfDBl2pGNbya7yxVio5wlDst27Lzuen','2008-09-07 01:08:21'),(1936,'<p style=\"color:red;\">Notice: Had another delay due to a version control explosion, \r\nbut the problem is fixed and a release will be made available later today 10/16/2008.\r\n<p style=\"color:red;\">This release was originally delayed to bring a more full featured environment to the open-source arena.\r\n\r\nMore information to come...rBQVKWfROtYLEGH2klUJydzrY5msaCRoOQfYj6a9cWQSqMgEtANpL56qYnaO','2008-09-07 01:11:07'),(4088,'You may be noticing erratic behavior at times, as I play \r\naround with the settings.\r\n<p>I hope to release a solid beta version soon \r\n(days, not weeks).  \r\n\r\n<p>Thank you.\r\n\r\n<p>P.S. Please feel free to play around.  \r\nCreate a login and add articles.  And if you \r\nlike you may also send me feedback \r\nat: <a href=\"mailto:daveb@softwaresensation.com\">\r\ndaveb@softwaresensation.com</a>\r\nnNPM8ahekAF5gHykMReDWK5Oa4I2YNmUPZquBldkFImoAKcvxQJmo4qci7IMoxLIK1a4urUtobsy8BlI','2008-10-02 20:14:38'),(4178,'<div style=\"background-color:red\">&nbsp;</div>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:#ddddff; padding:10px;\">\r\n    \r\n        <ul style=\"font-weight:bold;\">\r\n            <li>Single- and Multi-User Blogging\r\n            <li>Database driven (JNDI/JDBC, can use any database)\r\n            <li>Popular, Fresh, Older, Rising and Activity Views\r\n            <li>Article Voting and Spam Flagging With/Without Login\r\n            <li>Article Commenting (with voting and spam flagging)\r\n            <li>Optionally Allows Anonymous Commenting\r\n            <li>Provides a Blogging API With Tags\r\n            <li>Simple JSP Templates\r\n            <li>Completely Configurable Look and Feel \r\n            <li>Large Number of Configuration Options\r\n            <li>Supports Multiple RSS Feed Types\r\n            <li>Can Import From External RSS Feeds\r\n            <li>Advanced Searching (uses Lucene for indexing)\r\n            <li>Search Results Can be RSS Feeds\r\n            <li>Search Results Can Also be Menu Items\r\n            <li>Uses Java 1.5-1.6 Concurrent Collections\r\n            <li>Uses Ehcache for Object Caching\r\n            <li>Tag Cloud (with normalization)\r\n            <li>New User Registration with Email Verification\r\n            <li>Can Define HTML That Is Allowed\r\n            <li>Administrator Role Access\r\n            <li>Extremely Server Friendly \r\n        </ul>\r\n    \r\n    </div>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:red\">&nbsp;</div>\r\n<br />\r\n<div align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"/images/jwablogger.gif\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" alt=\"jwaBlogger\" />\r\n</div>\r\n<br />\r\nuD7HB2RKUkqnGA5ulwfys46JzjK3b8l4DKvTIMFS2GCLJ9eXRzBNs0u1nl8TybtGKaT895jw3fU2OQJZ','2008-10-05 13:49:01'),(4480,'<div style=\"background-color:red\">&nbsp;</div>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:#ddddff; padding:10px;\">\r\n    <ul />\r\n        <li>jndiName = jdbc/jwablogger\r\n        <li>debug = FINE\r\n        <li>entries = 500\r\n        <li>popularEntries = 50\r\n        <li>activityQueueSize = 40\r\n        <li>risingQueueSize = 40\r\n        <li>tagCloudSize = 40\r\n        <li>minTagCloudFontSize = 11\r\n        <li>maxTagCloudFontSize = 21\r\n        <li>invisibleTimeout = 600000\r\n        <li>feedSize = 50\r\n        <li>searchSize = 100\r\n        <li>pageSize = 10\r\n        <li>useSimpleLogin = false\r\n        <li>emailHost = localhost\r\n        <li>#emailUser = optional\r\n        <li>#emailPassword = optional\r\n        <li>emailFrom = verify@jwablogger.com\r\n        <li>emailFromName = jwaBlogger Verify\r\n        <li>allowNewUsers = true\r\n        <li>allowCookieLogin = true\r\n        <li>allowAnonymousVoting = true\r\n        <li>allowAnonymousSpamVoting = true\r\n        <li>spamThreshold = 20\r\n        <li>commentSpamThreshold = 10\r\n        <li>indexDirectory = luceneIndex\r\n        <li>addDescriptionToIndex = true\r\n        <li>addEntryToIndex = true\r\n        <li>indexTimerInterval = 5000\r\n        <li>tagCloudTimerInterval = 600000\r\n        <li>entryTimerInterval = 5000\r\n        <li>popularTimerInterval = 600000\r\n        <li>feedTimerInterval = 3600000\r\n        <li>feedTitle = jwaBlogger - \r\n        <li>feedDescription = jwaBlogger is Software Sensation\'s open-source blogging/links environment.\r\n        <li>feedAuthor = David Bulmore\r\n        <li>feedCopyright = Copyright(C) 2002 - present.  David Bulmore/Software Sensation Inc.  ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.\r\n        <li>feedEntryDelay = 5000\r\n    <ul />\r\n</div>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:red\">&nbsp;</div>\r\n<br />\r\n<div align=\"center\">\r\n<img src=\"/images/jwablogger.gif\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" alt=\"jwaBlogger\" />\r\n</div>\r\n<br />xCEWG9Oo4i9bohNFvWjHcEi3kLTWlIC1eo87fM0layUgtOr2HuXlZQjay9NpOWaVplvqAj83H15pzLBu','2008-10-09 02:20:56'),(4485,'<div style=\"background-color:#efefef; margin:5px; padding:5px 10px; border:4px solid #aaaaaa;\">\r\n<p>jwaBlogger is a full-featured links/blogging environment that can be used as a \r\npersonal blogger or as a full fledged multi-user link and blogging social network \r\nor anything you can imagine.  jwaBlogger can easily be customized to your needs \r\nusing just CSS and simple JSP tags.  jwaBlogger also provides a programmer\'s API \r\nand a set of easy to use tags that can be placed in any of your JSP.\r\n\r\n<p>jwaBlogger also provides the following APIs for use by Java developers:\r\n\r\n<p><b>BlogManager</b>\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n    <li>close();\r\n    <li>getProperties();\r\n    <li>getDatabaseManager();\r\n    <li>getBlogEntries();\r\n    <li>getPopularBlogEntries();\r\n    <li>getRisingBlogEntries();\r\n    <li>getActiveBlogEntries();\r\n    <li>getTagCloud();\r\n    <li>getBlogEntryById();\r\n    <li>getBlogEntryByName();\r\n    <li>getBlogEntryText();\r\n    <li>getBlogEntryComments();\r\n    <li>getBlogEntryComment();\r\n    <li>saveBlogEntry();\r\n    <li>saveBlogComment();\r\n    <li>writeFeed();\r\n    <li>updateBlogVotes();\r\n    <li>updateBlogSpam();\r\n    <li>updateCommentVotes();\r\n    <li>updateCommentSpam();\r\n    <li>search();\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p><b>Tag Library</b>\r\n\r\n<ul>  \r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:blogEntryDetail /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:blogEntryForm /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:blogEntryList /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:blogEntrySearchForm /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:blogEntryBody /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:cloud /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:commentForm /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:comments /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:login /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:menu /&gt;\r\n    <li>&lt;jwaBlogger:registrationForm /&gt;\r\n</ul>\r\n</div>y3mkSU6j9esITutoqYJ6nztvwQapMj5kLjoVHqdJdYSnzxFUP4o8i6c0aYlkyMvhtBQXgjJGPTkMUvLR','2008-10-09 02:38:16'),(4963,'<div>\r\n<h1>Getting Started with jwaBlogger</h1>\r\n\r\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>jwaBlogger is an easy to configure, setup,\r\ncustomize, and use social links and blogging environment similar to\r\nsites like Digg, Buzzup, DZone, The Server Side, Slashdot,\r\netc.&nbsp; jwaBlogger also makes for a nice personal blogging\r\nenvironment.&nbsp; jwaBlogger has tons of features.&nbsp;\r\nIncluding:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>Popular, Fresh, Older, Rising and Activity\r\nViews</li>\r\n<li>Article Voting and Spam Flagging With/Without\r\nLogin</li>\r\n<li>Article Commenting (with voting and spam\r\nflagging)</li>\r\n<li>Optionally Allows Anonymous Commenting</li>\r\n<li>Completely Configurable Look and Feel</li>\r\n<li>Large Number of Configuration Options</li>\r\n<li>Supports Multiple RSS Feed Types</li>\r\n<li>Can Import From External RSS Feeds</li>\r\n<li>Advanced Searching (uses Lucene for\r\nindexing)</li>\r\n<li>Uses Ehcache for Object Caching</li>\r\n<li>Tag Cloud (with name normalization)</li>\r\n<li>New User Registration with Email\r\nVerification</li>\r\n<li>Can Define Allowed HTML</li>\r\n<li>Administrator Role Access</li>\r\n<li>Extremely Server Friendly</li>\r\n<li>WYSIWYG HTML Editing</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>Installation:</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>Of course, the goal is for jwaBlogger to be an\r\nextremely easy environment to install, customize and use.&nbsp; To\r\nthis end we currently provide a MySQL version of jwaBlogger, and an\r\nembedded database version (next couple of days).&nbsp; You can also\r\nuse any other database by simply creating the database and pointing\r\nto it via JNDI.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Installing jwaBlogger is simply a matter of\r\ndropping the jwaBlogger.war file into your servlet\r\ncontainer&rsquo;s (Tomcat, Resin) webapps directory.&nbsp; In the\r\ncase of the embedded version you&rsquo;re done.&nbsp; Simply start\r\nthe server and type <a href=\r\n\"http://localhost:8080/jwablogger\">http://localhost:8080/jwablogger</a>\r\nand you should see what you see at <a href=\r\n\"http://www.jwablogger.org/\">http://www.jwablogger.org</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>In the case of the MySQL version, you first\r\nneed to create the database in MySQL.&nbsp; If you haven&rsquo;t\r\nalready, download the jwablogger.sql file.&nbsp; Then simply import\r\nthe database into MySQL:</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>mysql -u\r\nuser_with_create_permissions -ppassword &lt; jwablogger.sql</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Then, create a new MySQL user and password\r\n(username=jwablogger, password=jwablogger), and assign it SELECT,\r\nINSERT, UPDATE and DELETE privileges for the jwablogger\r\ndatabase.&nbsp; If your database server is accessible to others,\r\nyou will want to change the username and password to something more\r\nsecure, but this also requires changing the JNDI definition.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>If using Tomcat you&rsquo;re good to go\r\n(Tomcat is predefined in the META-INF directory).&nbsp; If\r\nyou&rsquo;re using Resin or another servlet container you may need\r\nto define a JNDI database resource.&nbsp; For resin this would\r\nbe:</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>&lt;database\r\njndi-name=\"jdbc/jwablogger\"&gt;</p>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n&lt;driver type=\"com.mysql.jdbc.Driver\"&gt;</p>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n&lt;url&gt;jdbc:mysql://localhost/jwablogger&lt;/url&gt;</p>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n&lt;user&gt;jwablogger&lt;/user&gt;</p>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>\r\n&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n&lt;password&gt;jwablogger&lt;/password&gt;</p>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;\r\n&lt;/driver&gt;</p>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>&lt;/database&gt;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>At this point jwaBlogger should be ready for\r\nuse. &nbsp;So, start the server and type <a href=\r\n\"http://localhost:8080/jwablogger\">http://localhost:8080/jwablogger</a>\r\nand you should see what you see at <a href=\r\n\"http://www.jwablogger.org/\">http://www.jwablogger.org</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Installing jwaBlogger as the default\r\napplication (runs as http://www.host.com) usually just requires\r\njwaBlogger being moved to the ROOT directory in the webapps\r\ndirectory.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Getting jwaBlogger to run under other\r\ncontainers may require more expertise, but it&rsquo;s a simple Java\r\nservlet and should be easy for Java oriented web developers.&nbsp;\r\nHowever, if you need assistance we are more then willing to provide\r\nsupport.</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>Customizing jwaBlogger</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>As mentioned the goal is for ease of\r\nuse.&nbsp; jwaBlogger was designed to be easily configured and\r\ncustomized via the following:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>jwaBlogger style sheets</li>\r\n<li>jwaBlogger.jsp (JSP based template)</li>\r\n<li>rightSide.jsp (Our right side example, which\r\nyou will likely want to replace)</li>\r\n<li>Using Tags (tag files) and a Simple API</li>\r\n<li>jwaBlogger properties and configuration\r\nfiles</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>The configuration and properties files are in\r\nthe WEB-INF directory and they are as follows:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>jwaBlogger.properties (a Java properties file\r\nthat provides configuration properties to both the Java API and to\r\nthe JSP via ${blogManager.properties.propertyName}), feel free to\r\nedit and/or add to it.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.25in\'>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>jwaBlogger.cloudTags (a little miss-leading,\r\nthese are the normalized names that are seen in the tag cloud.\r\nexample: Java, java).</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.25in\'>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>jwaBlogger.feeds (optional external feeds\r\nthat are imported into jwaBlogger and it&rsquo;s database).</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.25in\'>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>jwaBlogger.htmlTags (you can control exactly\r\nwhat is and is not allowed for HTML content).</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>NOTE: It is not intended that any of the tag\r\nfiles be modified for simple changes, but you can modify them if\r\nyou like (tag files are likely to change over time).&nbsp; All the\r\ntag files are simple and can easily be copied and modified.&nbsp;\r\nFor example, if you want different menu options simply copy\r\nWEB-INF/tags/jwaBlogger/menu.tag to WEB-INF/menu.jsp make your\r\nchanges and include it in the jwaBlogger.jsp template:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>&lt;jsp:include\r\npage=&rdquo;/WEB-INF/menu.jsp&rdquo; /&gt;</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<h2>jwaBlogger.properties (and their defaults)</h2>\r\n\r\n<p># JNDI Name - should not have to change\r\nthis</p>\r\n<p>jndiName = jdbc/jwablogger</p>\r\n\r\n<p># debug level - see java.utils.logging</p>\r\n<p>debug = WARNING</p>\r\n\r\n<p># If true, the article will show in the list\r\n(traditional blog)</p>\r\n<p>includeArticleInEntry = false</p>\r\n\r\n<p># maximum entries to keep in memory/cached</p>\r\n<p>entries = 500</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Number to show in popular view</p>\r\n<p>popularEntries = 50</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Number to show in activity view</p>\r\n<p>activityQueueSize = 40</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Number to show in rising view</p>\r\n<p>risingQueueSize = 40</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Number of tags to show in tag cloud</p>\r\n<p>tagCloudSize = 40</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Starting tag font size</p>\r\n<p>minTagCloudFontSize = 11</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Maximum tag font size</p>\r\n<p>maxTagCloudFontSize = 21</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Entries can be invisible for a limited time\r\nbefore they are pulled from the queues permenantly</p>\r\n<p>invisibleTimeout = 600000</p>\r\n\r\n<p># The number of entries provided in a feed\r\n(rss-0.9 auto limits to 15)</p>\r\n<p>feedSize = 50</p>\r\n\r\n<p># The maximum number returned for a search</p>\r\n<p>searchSize = 100</p>\r\n\r\n<p># the default number of entries per page</p>\r\n<p>pageSize = 10</p>\r\n\r\n<p># If true a single textbox is used for\r\nusername:passwd</p>\r\n<p>useSimpleLogin = false</p>\r\n\r\n<p># If email verification is used this is the\r\nemail server host</p>\r\n<p>emailHost = localhost</p>\r\n<p>#emailUser = optional</p>\r\n<p>#emailPassword = optional</p>\r\n\r\n<p># who&rsquo;s the email comming from</p>\r\n<p>emailFrom = <a href=\r\n\"mailto:verify@jwablogger.com\">verify@jwablogger.com</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p># who&rsquo;s the email comming from by\r\nname</p>\r\n<p>emailFromName = jwaBlogger Verify</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Set to false for a single article\r\nsubmitter</p>\r\n<p>allowNewUsers = true</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Allow returning users to be logged in\r\nautomatically</p>\r\n<p>allowCookieLogin = true</p>\r\n\r\n<p># If false, users must login to vote</p>\r\n<p>allowAnonymousVoting = true</p>\r\n\r\n<p># If false, users must login to spam vote</p>\r\n<p>allowAnonymousSpamVoting = true</p>\r\n\r\n<p># users are mean and will spam just for fun so\r\nset this as needed</p>\r\n<p>minSpamCount = 5</p>\r\n\r\n<p># The maximum spam votes allowed before the\r\narticle is pulled</p>\r\n<p>maxSpamCount = 10</p>\r\n\r\n<p># users are mean and will spam just for fun so\r\nset this as needed</p>\r\n<p>minCommentSpamCount = 5</p>\r\n\r\n<p># The maximum spam votes allowed before the\r\ncomment is pulled</p>\r\n<p>maxCommentSpamCount = 10</p>\r\n\r\n<p># the directory for the Lucene search\r\nindex</p>\r\n<p>indexDirectory = luceneIndex</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Add the article description to the index</p>\r\n<p>addDescriptionToIndex = true</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Add the article entry text to the index\r\n(could be large)</p>\r\n<p>addEntryToIndex = true</p>\r\n\r\n<p># The index and entry queues should be\r\nprocessed fairly regularly - How often are articles submitted?</p>\r\n<p>indexTimerInterval = 15000</p>\r\n<p>entryTimerInterval = 15000</p>\r\n\r\n<p># How often do you want the tag cloud to\r\nupdate?</p>\r\n<p>tagCloudTimerInterval = 3600000</p>\r\n\r\n<p># How often do you want the popular list to\r\nupdate?</p>\r\n<p>popularTimerInterval = 3600000</p>\r\n\r\n<p># How often do you want to read external\r\nfeeds?</p>\r\n<p>feedTimerInterval = 3600000</p>\r\n\r\n<p># RSS feed title</p>\r\n<p>feedTitle = jwaBlogger -</p>\r\n\r\n<p># RSS feed description</p>\r\n<p>feedDescription = jwaBlogger is Software\r\nSensation\'s open-source blogging/links environment.</p>\r\n\r\n<p># RSS feed Author - the one hosting the site\r\nand/or submitting articles</p>\r\n<p>feedAuthor = David Bulmore</p>\r\n\r\n<p># Normal copyright stuff</p>\r\n<p>feedCopyright = Copyright(C) 2002 -\r\npresent.&nbsp; David Bulmore/Software Sensation Inc.&nbsp; ALL\r\nRIGHTS RESERVED.</p>\r\n\r\n<p># when using external feeds you can delay the\r\nentries as they come into jwaBlogger</p>\r\n<p>feedEntryDelay = 10000</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>jwaBlogger.cloudTags</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>The cloud tags file is used to normalize the\r\ncloud tags as seen in the cloud.&nbsp; It simply has the\r\nformat:</p>\r\n\r\n<p style=\'margin-left:.5in\'>normalized tag name[,\r\nunnormalized tag name, ...]</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The normalized tag name also covers character\r\ncase, so only one word is usually needed</p>\r\n\r\n<p># normalized tag[, unnormalized tag, ...]</p>\r\n<p>Agile</p>\r\n<p>Ajax</p>\r\n<p>...</p>\r\n<p>Books, Book, Book-Review</p>\r\n<p>...</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>jwaBlogger.feeds</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>External feeds can be imported to\r\njwaBlogger.&nbsp; In most cases only the title, link, author,\r\ndescription information is included, the article itself is usually\r\nnot included (jRoller is an exception).</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The format is a &lsquo;|&rsquo; separated list\r\nwith the following definitions:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>Name - The name of the feed is also the\r\nauthor displayed for the entry</li>\r\n<li>Tags - The tags that are added to the article\r\nentry</li>\r\n<li>Use content - If false the description will\r\nbe used as the content</li>\r\n<li>allowed tags - Include article if it has one\r\nof these tags</li>\r\n<li>Feed URL - the feed URL same as used in an\r\nRSS client</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p># Name | Tags | Use content | allowed tags |\r\nFeed URL</p>\r\n<p>\r\nDZone|DZone|false|java|http://www.dzone.com/links/feed/frontpage/rss.xml</p>\r\n<p>\r\njRoller|jRoller|true|java|http://www.jroller.com/frontpage/feed/entries/rss</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>jwaBlogger.htmlTags</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>The html tags file provides tons of power in\r\nwhat you will and won&rsquo;t allow your users to use for HTML\r\ncontent.&nbsp; All HTML is normalized and only the tags and\r\nattributes defined in the html tags file are allowed.&nbsp; Any\r\ntags or attributes not defined will simply be ignored (but not the\r\ninner HTML or text).</p>\r\n\r\n<p># HTML tag[, allowed attributes[, ...]]</p>\r\n<p>abbr, id, class, style, title, dir, lang</p>\r\n<p>acronym, id, class, style, title, dir,\r\nlang</p>\r\n<p>address, id, class, style, title, dir,\r\nlang</p>\r\n<p>b, id, class, style, title, dir, lang</p>\r\n<p>...</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>Administration</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>One of the best features of jwaBlogger is the\r\nfact that it is mostly self-managing.&nbsp; There is not a whole\r\nlot of anything that has to be done outside of normal server and\r\ndatabase activities.&nbsp; However, there are some things an\r\nadministrator needs to do, such as editing unwanted content and\r\nremoving spam.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>With jwaBlogger spam can be managed by the\r\nusers.&nbsp; As users spam vote an article/comment, a minimum\r\nthreshold may be reached at which time the article/comment will be\r\nmarked as spam.&nbsp; As more users spam vote, the spam will\r\neventually reach a maximum threshold and the article/comment will\r\nbe removed.&nbsp; However, if an administrator spams an article, it\r\nwill be given the maximum number and removed from all queues.&nbsp;\r\nAn administrator can also, at anytime, edit any article and/or make\r\nit not visible.&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n<p>NOTE: Reaching maximum spam and/or marking an\r\narticle invisible only affects their ability to be in the queues,\r\nbut they can still come up in searches.&nbsp; So, if an article is\r\noffensive, it should be edited and the offensive content\r\nremoved.&nbsp; However, comments are not searchable and will not be\r\nseen.</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Managing Users</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>User management is also self-managed.&nbsp;\r\nHowever, to define a trusted user (users who can enter HTML content\r\nunchecked), or to define an administrator, you have to edit the\r\nusers table directly.&nbsp; There is no way to change the\r\nadministrator/trusted status via the web interface.&nbsp; You can\r\nalso disable a user account by deleting it and/or changing the\r\npassword (adding a single character to the front of the encrypted\r\npassword is very effective).</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Database</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>The database is simple, and while referential\r\nintegrity exists between article related tables, there is none\r\nassociated with the user table.&nbsp; This may change in the\r\nfuture, but for now the reasons are as follows:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>Feeds submit articles and we don&rsquo;t want\r\nto create a user to have a feed.</li>\r\n<li>We have no need for cascading deletes and\r\nupdates.</li>\r\n<li>and, we&rsquo;re not keeping statistics on\r\nuser activities.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>Changes can be made to the database tables\r\nand/or their data (providing table names and column names are\r\nwithin jPersist&rsquo;s mapping abilities) at anytime.&nbsp; You\r\ncan also change defaults, data types (within reason and acceptable\r\nbounds), and data size with no ill-effects to jPersist and/or\r\njwaBlogger.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>However, if you change article data (clean the\r\ndatabase, update searchable columns, etc), then the search index\r\nshould be rebuilt (see next section).</p>\r\n\r\n<h3>Search Index</h3>\r\n\r\n<p>jwaBlogger uses Apache&rsquo;s Lucene for\r\nsearch indexing.&nbsp; If the index gets out of sync with the\r\ndatabase (via updates and/or deletes), your searches can start\r\nreturning odd results.&nbsp; If this happens, it&rsquo;s time to\r\nrebuild the index.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Rebuilding the index happens when the index\r\ndoes not exist, so simply delete the index directory and/or all the\r\nfiles in it.</p>\r\n\r\n<h2>The Future of jwaBlogger</h2>\r\n\r\n<p>We&rsquo;ll be adding much more in the weeks\r\nto come.&nbsp; Some of the features being added include:</p>\r\n\r\n<ul style=\'margin-top:0in\' type=\"disc\">\r\n<li>Look and feel templates</li>\r\n<li>Ability to edit comments</li>\r\n<li>The option to allow down voting (for those\r\nwho want to be less civilized)</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n<p>More to come...&nbsp;</p>\r\n\r\n</div>\r\nSA9U3JerzNho4mXx0y3AsRr8XKplb7OqshrKPUE4ZYkuxvOECtl2Jd8p91vb','2008-10-17 03:43:39'),(5435,'<p>jwaBlogger 1.0.2 has been released.  It includes several \r\nimprovements in queue and feed management.  Also fixed \r\nsearch result as RSS feed.</p>\r\n\r\n28fyvt5nzI9ZwxhJjelt3gVHSEq8NWl8yCioR65grTkSfNnVI6MxeQXlHIS6','2008-10-28 13:30:08'),(5436,'gYsqMfF3AV0tZUsRmeLW98KxilPVvMBIdq8HC0yxRC48NjlDuhZSUHlwADucmBkFdrcvAS','2025-10-21 07:57:04'),(5437,'u8Ny4QEasinXPN8vG2uVpVOo6s2WLPiBZOVIvSsAXpORBqxliu5vXbYAtEgwo4VNSDfKkb','2025-10-21 07:57:12'),(5438,'lpO0S12NxTrtaVTqNK9XFHQJG7slMixSc1dnZKMgTEHZGIJ6Nj','2025-11-04 03:06:44'),(5439,'D9PeO7kKxrlDAVpLmBRZ8ODIxBFLCuwzPlgMqZJpedt8E1rqzA','2025-11-04 03:06:52'),(5440,'vQY0a8UBb4Bd7tzexsir7NLlqZn54Y','2025-12-02 03:16:23'),(5441,'vx2Q8soKTGSesCba3ATzFmjJ9ofBwU','2025-12-02 03:16:32'),(5442,'5Ig439bEkPaHjGsw52fy1Dw7eBYLCj','2025-12-02 03:27:37'),(5443,'S3ZH24sIyrxNuoIMcmyH0pkj8g6xtq','2025-12-02 03:27:45');
